1
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:15,560
Android's permission model is a central component of its security architecture, governing how applications

2
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,480
access sensitive resources and data tool.

3
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:25,120
It's a fine grained system designed to protect user privacy and device security by requiring applications

4
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:27,880
to explicitly declare the permissions they need.

5
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:32,520
Understanding this permission model is crucial for both developers and users.

6
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:37,680
The foundation of the Android permission model lies in the principle of least privilege.

7
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:43,120
Applications are only granted the permissions necessary to perform their intended functions, minimizing

8
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:46,120
the potential damage if an application is compromised.

9
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:52,040
This helps prevent malicious applications from gaining unauthorized access to sensitive data or system

10
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:52,840
resources.

11
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:57,600
Prior to Android six, marshmallow permissions were granted at install time.

12
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:03,640
Users were presented with a list of permissions, an application requested, and had to accept them

13
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:05,040
all to install the app.

14
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:11,560
This approach lacked granularity and transparency, as users often didn't understand the implications

15
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:13,520
of granting certain permissions.

16
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:19,280
With the introduction of runtime permissions in Android six marshmallow, the permission model underwent

17
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:20,880
a significant change.

18
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:27,240
Now, applications request permissions at runtime when they actually need to access a sensitive resource.

19
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:32,960
This gives users more control over what applications can access, and allows them to make more informed

20
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:35,200
decisions about granting permissions.

21
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:39,920
Runtime permissions are grouped into categories based on the type of resource they protect.

22
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:45,640
For example, the location permission group includes permissions related to accessing the device's location.

23
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:52,040
When an application requests a permission from a group, the user is prompted to grant or deny the permission.

24
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:57,800
If the user grants the permission, the application is allowed to access any resource protected by that

25
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:58,680
permission group.

26
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,400
Custom permissions are defined by individual applications to protect their own resources and data.

27
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:09,720
These permissions are not part of the standard Android permissions set and are specific to the application

28
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:10,640
that defines them.

29
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:16,480
Other applications can request access to these custom permissions in order to interact with the defining

30
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:18,240
application's resources.

31
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:24,320
Custom permissions provide a way for applications to control access to their data and functionality,

32
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:28,110
allowing them to limit access to only trusted applications.

33
00:02:28,550 --> 00:02:33,350
The Android permission model also supports the concept of signature permissions.

34
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:38,750
Signature permissions are granted to applications that are signed with the same certificate.

35
00:02:39,070 --> 00:02:44,550
This allows applications from the same developer to share resources and data without requiring explicit

36
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:45,550
user consent.

37
00:02:45,910 --> 00:02:51,630
Signature permissions are often used by system applications and other trusted applications to access

38
00:02:51,630 --> 00:02:53,390
sensitive system resources.

39
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:57,750
It's also important to highlight that the permission model is continuously evolving.

40
00:02:58,630 --> 00:03:04,870
Google regularly introduces new permissions and updates the existing ones to address emerging security

41
00:03:04,910 --> 00:03:07,230
threats and privacy concerns.

42
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:13,630
For example, Android ten introduced new restrictions on background location access, giving users even

43
00:03:13,630 --> 00:03:17,430
more control over how applications use their location data.

44
00:03:18,430 --> 00:03:23,550
All in all, the Android permission model is a crucial aspect of the operating system's security.

45
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:29,550
It controls how applications request access to data, resources, and functionalities.

